## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a structured CAPA for a real-world, beginner-friendly scenario with multi-region impact and a concrete data model.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data modeling for multi-region telemetry
- Simple lifecycle/state machine for containment
- Minimal REST API design to create/update CAPAs with evidence
- Region-aware metrics to prove containment
- RCA template and staged remediation (canary, quotas)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API sketch (Express)
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  // create CAPA skeleton
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'CAPA-001' });
});
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate CAPA effectiveness before closing it? 
- What data would you collect for RCA if the spike recurs in a new region?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design practical CAPA governance tied to real-time data flows and multi-tenant risk without overengineering.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model fields (id, tenant_ids, sink, data_classification, evidence, region, remediation_links, state)
- Region-aware lifecycle states
- Minimal REST API (POST /caps, PATCH /caps/{id})
- Metrics: recurrence_rate, MTTR, false_positives, containment_time per region
- RCA template and safe-canary rollback plan

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express endpoints for CAPA creation
const app = require('express')();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => { /* create CAPA */ res.status(201).send({ id: 'capa-123' }); });
app.patch('/caps/:id', (req, res) => { /* update CAPA */ res.status(200).send({ ok: true }); });
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a CAPA program for data privacy incidents in a multi-tenant, real-time pipeline, balancing compliance and engineering practicality.

## Key Concepts
- Data lineage and PII tagging across streaming pipelines
- Region-aware metrics and containment strategies
- Minimal REST API design for CAPA lifecycle
- RCA templates plus staged preventive actions (canaries)
- Versioned redaction policy rollbacks and automated checks

## Code Example
```javascript
type CAPARecord = {
  id: string;
  evidenceRefs: string[];
  dataLineage: string;
  redactionVersion: string;
  implicatedRegions: string[];
  tenantIds: string[];
  severity: string;
  status: 'detected'|'contained'|'verified'|'closed';
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt?: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the effectiveness of the redaction rollback in production?
- Which metrics would you surface to product and compliance teams during a breach incident?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests ability to design a production-grade CAPA for a multi-tenant data platform, emphasizing isolation, auditability, and scalable rollout.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped CAPA data model with evidence and artifacts
- Immutable links to logs/traces and resource metadata
- Lifecycle state machine and canary-based enforcement
- Per-tenant metrics (recurrence, MTTR, spillover rate)

## Code Example
```javascript
// TypeScript interface for CAPA
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  tenantId: string;
  policyId: string;
  incidentId: string;
  evidenceLinks: string[];
  artifacts: string[];
  affectedResources: string[];
  dataClassification: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle CAPA versioning and backward compatibility?
- How to simulate cross-tenant leakage in a canary without impacting production?

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design CAPA for security-critical cross-region encryption failures, including evidence capture and regulatory considerations.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model capturing evidence and artifacts
- Region-aware lifecycle with multi-region coordination
- Minimal REST API for CAPAs with logs and traces
- Metrics for containment, MTTR, recurrence, false positives
- RCA templates and canary rollout strategies for secure rotation

## Code Example
```javascript
type CAPA = {
  id: string;
  incidentId: string;
  regions: string[];
  keyId: string;
  artifacts: string[];
  logs: string[];
  evidenceLinks: string[];
  status: string;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
